import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/TestingInbox
import ../../../Demos/TestingAnswers
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Feature
import ../../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/Specimen
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources

## The testing package: a first test in Roc (a pure component, its expects, and what the queries
## answer, asked as this page renders), the four ways to test an app, the queries, then the same
## queries from Node against the app's server, a database of a test's own and a real browser; the
## API; and what waits on the platform. Static: nothing on it hydrates.
meta = {
	title: "Testing",
	description: "Tests that find things in a page as a person does, by role and name, label and text: in Roc expects, and from node:test against the app's server, a database of the test's own and a real browser.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "first", label: "A first test" },
		{ id: "ways", label: "Four ways to test" },
		{ id: "queries", label: "The queries" },
		{ id: "dom", label: "Dom, in Roc" },
		{ id: "node", label: "From Node" },
		{ id: "database", label: "A database, and jobs" },
		{ id: "browser", label: "In a browser" },
		{ id: "javascript", label: "The JavaScript" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<NoHydration>
		<PageHero
			kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/testing</code>]}
			title="Tests that read a page as a person does"
		>
			Helpers for testing a Roc Solid app. A test finds a heading, a link by its name and a field by its
			label, as someone using the page finds them, not by the page's classes. In Roc, an
			<code>expect</code> renders a component and asks it. From Node, a test builds the app, starts its
			server, asks it as a browser would, reads the mail it sent, gives itself a database, and uses the
			page in a real browser.
		</PageHero>

		<Heading id="first">A first test</Heading>
		<p>
			A pure component (<code>view</code>, no <code>!</code>) makes its markup from its props alone. So an
			<code>expect</code> can render it to HTML with <code>Html.to_str</code>, read that with
			<code>Dom.parse</code>, and ask it what a person would see. Here is a small inbox, and the inbox as
			it renders:
		</p>
		<Specimen file={Sources.testing_inbox.file} code={[<Code source={Sources.testing_inbox.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]} label="As it renders (a picture: it does nothing here)">
			<div inert><TestingInbox name="Ada" unread={["Lunch?", "Re: the plan"]} /></div>
		</Specimen>
		<p>
			Its tests are in the same file, under it. <code>roc test</code> runs them with every other
			<code>expect</code> the app has, and this site's tests run these:
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_inbox_expects.file} source={Sources.testing_inbox_expects.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		<p>The app names the package in its header, by its directory's name, as it names any package:</p>
		<Code source={"app [program] { testing: \"../packages/testing/main.roc\", pf: platform \"…\" }"} numbers={Bool.False} />
		<p>
			A query that finds one element answers <code>Ok</code> with it. One that finds none, or more than one,
			says so, and says what there is instead, so a failing test tells you what went wrong. This is what
			<code>page.get</code> answers for a few queries of the inbox above, asked as this page was rendered:
		</p>
		<TableWrap><TestingAnswers /></TableWrap>
		<details>
			<summary>How the table is made: <code>{Sources.testing_answers.file}</code></summary>
			<Code title={Sources.testing_answers.file} source={Sources.testing_answers.rocx} />
		</details>

		<Heading id="ways">Four ways to test</Heading>
		<FeatureGrid>
			<Feature title="Rules and pure components, in Roc">
				<p>
					An <code>expect</code> beside the code. <code>Html.to_str</code> renders a pure component, and
					<code>Dom.parse</code> reads it. Fast, typed, and run by <code>roc test</code>.
				</p>
			</Feature>
			<Feature title="The app, over HTTP, from Node">
				<p>
					<code>node:test</code> builds the app as a deploy does, starts its server on a port of its own,
					and asks it as a browser would: pages, forms, server functions, events, the mail it sent.
				</p>
			</Feature>
			<Feature title="Its database and its jobs">
				<p>
					Each test gets a Postgres database of its own, which the server migrates. A test waits until the
					jobs it caused have run, and sees how each ended.
				</p>
			</Feature>
			<Feature title="A real browser">
				<p>
					Chromium's headless shell. A page hydrates strictly, and a test clicks and types in it as a person
					does. A component with signals is tested through its story.
				</p>
			</Feature>
		</FeatureGrid>

		<Heading id="queries">The queries</Heading>
		<p>
			The same queries in Roc and in JavaScript, with the same rules. The JavaScript ones have Testing
			Library's names, so a test reads as one written for React or Solid would.
		</p>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>In Roc</th><th>In JavaScript</th><th>Finds</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><code>Role(role)</code></td>
						<td><code>getByRole(role)</code></td>
						<td>
							elements with that ARIA role: their own (<code>role="tab"</code>), or their tag's: a
							<code>{"<button>"}</code> is a button, an <code>{"<a href>"}</code> a link, an <code>{"<h2>"}</code> a
							heading, a <code>{"<ul>"}</code> a list, an <code>{"<input type=\"checkbox\">"}</code> a checkbox
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>Named(role, name)</code></td>
						<td><code>{"getByRole(role, { name })"}</code></td>
						<td>
							and whose accessible name is <code>name</code>: its <code>aria-labelledby</code>, its
							<code>aria-label</code>, its <code>{"<label>"}</code>, its <code>alt</code>, its text (a button's, a
							link's, a heading's), its <code>title</code>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr><td><code>Text(text)</code></td><td><code>getByText(text)</code></td><td>elements whose own text is <code>text</code>, not their children's</td></tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>Label(text)</code></td>
						<td><code>getByLabelText(text)</code></td>
						<td>
							the field a <code>{"<label>"}</code> with that text names (by <code>for</code>, or around it), or
							whose <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code> says it
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr><td><code>TestId(id)</code></td><td><code>getByTestId(id)</code></td><td>the element with <code>data-testid</code> set to <code>id</code></td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Tag(tag)</code>, <code>Attr(name, value)</code></td><td></td><td>elements by tag, or by an attribute's value, hidden or not</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>
		<p>
			Text is compared as a reader sees it: whitespace collapsed, and trimmed. Roles, names, text and labels
			skip what a reader can't see: an element that is <code>hidden</code>, <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>
			or <code>display: none</code> in its <code>style</code>, and what is inside it; <code>{"<head>"}</code>,
			<code>{"<script>"}</code>, <code>{"<style>"}</code>, <code>{"<template>"}</code>. In JavaScript a name or a text may also be a
			<code>RegExp</code>, and <code>getByRole</code> takes a heading's <code>level</code>.
		</p>

		<Heading id="dom">Dom, in Roc</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>Dom.parse(html)</code> reads HTML into a tree. It reads HTML that closes its elements, as Roc Solid
			writes it and a browser serializes it. A query on an element looks inside that element, and two
			<code>Dom</code>s compare with <code>==</code>.
		</p>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>page.get(query)</code></td><td><code>{"Try(Dom, [NotFound(Str), TooMany(Str)])"}</code>: the one element, or why not</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>page.first(query)</code>, <code>page.all(query)</code></td><td>the first; every one, in the page's order</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>page.contains(query)</code>, <code>page.count(query)</code></td><td>whether it finds any; how many</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>page.text_of(query)</code>, <code>page.texts(query)</code></td><td>the text of the one; of each</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>node.text()</code></td><td>what a reader reads in it, whitespace collapsed</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>node.attr(name)</code>, <code>node.tag()</code>, <code>node.has_class(class)</code></td><td>an attribute (<code>Err(Missing)</code> when it has none); its tag; whether it has a class</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>page.roles()</code></td><td>every role and name a reader can reach, to print with <code>dbg</code> when a query finds nothing</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>

		<Heading id="node">From Node</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>buildForTests</code> builds the app as a deploy does: <code>rocx build</code>, the browser's wasm
			and the server. <code>startServer</code> starts that server on a free port. A test file starts one for
			all its tests, and stops it at the end:
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_node_setup.file} source={Sources.testing_node_setup.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		<p>
			<code>app.client()</code> is someone at a browser, with a cookie jar of their own. Each answer has its
			<code>status</code>, its <code>text</code>, and a <code>dom</code> with the queries:
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_node_page.file} source={Sources.testing_node_page.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		<p>
			<code>post</code> sends a form as a browser does without the runtime: the endpoint answers with a redirect
			back to the page, and a flash that holds its result. A test sees the redirect, and reads the flash.
			<code>submit</code> sends the form as the runtime does, and gets the endpoint's answer.
			<code>call</code> calls a server function by its name, as <code>rocx routes</code> lists it, and
			<code>events</code> reads an endpoint's events as they come.
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_node_form.file} source={Sources.testing_node_form.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		<p>
			With no <code>--smtp-url</code> the server keeps the mail it sends. <code>waitForMail</code> waits for a
			message, and <code>links</code> finds the links in it:
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_node_mail.file} source={Sources.testing_node_mail.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

		<Heading id="database">A database, and jobs</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>testDatabase()</code> makes a database of the test's own, in the Postgres at
			<code>DATABASE_URL</code>, and <code>drop()</code> removes it. The server runs its migrations into it when
			it starts. <code>settleJobs()</code> waits until every job that is due has run, and the jobs those
			queued, and answers how each ended. A job that fails is tried again later; <code>{"{ later: true }"}</code>
			tries it now, until its attempts are spent. <code>enqueue</code> queues a job as a cron would at its time.
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_node_jobs.file} source={Sources.testing_node_jobs.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

		<Heading id="browser">In a browser</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>launchBrowser()</code> starts Chromium's headless shell, or gives <code>null</code> when there is
			none, so the test can skip. A tab waits until the page has hydrated, strictly: a page that isn't what the
			browser's half renders throws. Its queries are the same, and each element it finds can be clicked, typed
			in and filled, through the browser's own input. <code>findBy…</code> looks again until the page shows
			what it asks for.
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.testing_node_browser.file} source={Sources.testing_node_browser.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		<p>
			<code>Html.to_str</code> can't render a component with signals: its holes run in the runtime. Its
			<code>{"<story>"}</code> can be tested instead. <code>{"buildForTests(dir, { dev: true })"}</code> serves
			each story as a page, at <code>{"storyPath(\"Counter\", \"At ten\")"}</code>, to fetch or to open.
		</p>

		<Heading id="javascript">The JavaScript</Heading>
		<p>
			Plain <code>.mjs</code> files with no dependencies, each usable on its own; <code>index.mjs</code> has all
			of them.
		</p>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Gives a test</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><code>dom.mjs</code></td>
						<td>
							<code>screen(html)</code>, or <code>parse(html)</code> and <code>within(node)</code>: the queries, each as <code>getBy…</code>
							(one, or it throws), <code>queryBy…</code> (one, or <code>null</code>), <code>getAllBy…</code> and
							<code>queryAllBy…</code>. A node has <code>text</code>, <code>attr(name)</code>, <code>role</code>,
							<code>name</code>, <code>hasClass(name)</code>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>server.mjs</code></td>
						<td>
							<code>{"startServer(executable, { statics, database, dev, flags, env })"}</code>; its
							<code>client()</code>, with <code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, <code>submit</code>,
							<code>json</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>call</code>, <code>events</code> and <code>flash</code>;
							<code>mail()</code>, <code>waitForMail</code>, <code>logs()</code>, <code>stop()</code>; and
							<code>links</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>storyPath</code>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>postgres.mjs</code></td>
						<td>
							<code>testDatabase()</code>: <code>url</code>, <code>query</code>, <code>{"sql`…\${value}`"}</code>
							(each value written as a literal), <code>jobs</code>, <code>enqueue</code>, <code>settleJobs</code>,
							<code>drop</code>. A client of its own, over Node's sockets
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>browser.mjs</code></td>
						<td>
							<code>launchBrowser()</code>, <code>{"browser.open(url, { strict, cookies })"}</code>: a tab with the
							queries, <code>findBy…</code>, <code>waitFor</code>, <code>errors</code>, <code>console</code>,
							<code>screenshot</code>; elements with <code>click</code>, <code>fill</code>, <code>type</code>,
							<code>press</code>, <code>value</code>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr><td><code>build.mjs</code></td><td><code>{"buildForTests(dir, { client, dev, opt })"}</code>: the app built; its server and the directories it serves</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>

		<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
		<p>
			What is above works today, and the package tests it: <code>roc test packages/testing/main.roc</code> runs
			<code>Dom</code>'s <code>expect</code>s, and <code>node --test</code> runs its JavaScript against a small app
			it builds, with Postgres and Chromium when they are there. Each of these waits on a change to the
			platform, written up in <code>packages/testing/PROPOSALS.md</code>:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>a clock a test sets, and randomness it seeds: a test mode for the server;</li>
			<li>outbound requests (<code>Http</code>, and <code>S3</code> from packages/s3 through it) answered by stubs, so a test makes no network calls;</li>
			<li>jobs and crons run by a request, with no SQL (today <code>settleJobs</code> waits on the database);</li>
			<li>emptying the dev mailbox between tests (today <code>waitForMail</code> skips the messages already there);</li>
			<li>endpoints, pages and server functions tested in Roc, answered inside the server, and a component with signals rendered in a Roc test;</li>
			<li>helpers in this package that run on the server, until a package can hold server-only modules.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Limits, for now:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>The tabs of one browser share cookies, so <code>open</code> clears them first.</li>
			<li>
				<code>Dom.parse</code> doesn't close elements that HTML leaves open: <code>{"<p>one<p>two"}</code> is
				one paragraph inside another.
			</li>
			<li>
				The JavaScript finds the Roc Solid repository it is in, or the one <code>ROCX_ROOT</code> names: it builds
				with its compiler and drives Chromium with its driver.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</NoHydration>

<style>
	/* A narrow screen scrolls a table sideways (TableWrap) rather than breaking a name or a call in two,
	   or squeezing what it says into a word a line. */
	td:not(:last-child) code {
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	td:last-child {
		min-width: 14rem;
	}
</style>
